## Converting `std::string` to `std::vector`

There are several ways to convert a `std::string` to a `std::vector`, depending on what you need.

---

### 1. `std::vector<char>` (Character Vector)

**Using Range Constructor (Recommended)**
```cpp
std::string str = "Hello, World!";
std::vector<char> vec(str.begin(), str.end());
```

**Using `assign()`**
```cpp
std::vector<char> vec;
vec.assign(str.begin(), str.end());
```

**Using `std::copy`**
```cpp
std::vector<char> vec(str.size());
std::copy(str.begin(), str.end(), vec.begin());
```

---

### 2. `std::vector<std::string>` (Split by Delimiter)

**Using `std::stringstream`**
```cpp
#include <sstream>

std::string str = "one two three";
std::vector<std::string> vec;
std::stringstream ss(str);
std::string token;

while (std::getline(ss, token, ' ')) {  // ' ' is the delimiter
    vec.push_back(token);
}
// Result: ["one", "two", "three"]
```

**Using `std::ranges` (C++20)**
```cpp
#include <ranges>

std::string str = "one,two,three";
auto split = str | std::views::split(',');

std::vector<std::string> vec;
for (auto&& part : split) {
    vec.emplace_back(part.begin(), part.end());
}
```

---

### 3. `std::vector<uint8_t>` (Byte Vector)

```cpp
std::string str = "Hello";
std::vector<uint8_t> vec(str.begin(), str.end());
```

---

### Quick Comparison

| Method | Use Case |
|--------|----------|
| Range constructor | Simple and idiomatic |
| `assign()` | When vector already exists |
| `stringstream` | Splitting by delimiter |
| `std::ranges` | Modern C++20 splitting |

---

### Full Example
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <sstream>

int main() {
    // String to vector<char>
    std::string str = "Hello";
    std::vector<char> charVec(str.begin(), str.end());

    // String to vector<string> (split by space)
    std::string sentence = "one two three";
    std::vector<std::string> words;
    std::stringstream ss(sentence);
    std::string word;
    while (ss >> word) {
        words.push_back(word);
    }

    return 0;
}
```

The **range constructor** `std::vector<char> vec(str.begin(), str.end())` is generally the most idiomatic and recommended approach for converting to a character vector.

